## Context

The unknown-flag rejection convention has been applied across three PRs
(0166, 0178, 0180) and still has residuals (0185). The pattern recurs
because there is no standing test that would catch a new command missing
the guard at PR time.

A parameterized shell test that iterates over every command and asserts
`erg <cmd> --bogus` exits non-zero with "unknown flag" on stderr would:
- Catch any new command added without the guard.
- Prevent regressions if the guard is accidentally removed.
- Make the convention self-enforcing rather than audit-dependent.

## Actions

1. Write `tests/test_unknown_flags.sh` that loops over every command in
   the commands registry and runs `erg <cmd> --bogus`, asserting rc != 0
   and stderr contains "unknown flag".
2. Exclude commands that legitimately take no flags and silently ignore
   args (if any), documenting the exclusion explicitly.
3. Add the test to the test runner (Makefile or test_pipeline).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `tests/test_unknown_flags.sh` passes for every guarded command
- [ ] The test is exercised by `make test`
- [ ] A new command without the guard causes the test to fail
